1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,214
The motor makes torque from current, but
the inverter can only set voltages.

2
00:00:05,254 --> 00:00:08,265
Between them sits the fastest loop in the
drive:

3
00:00:08,305 --> 00:00:13,114
20 000 times a second, it measures the
currents and sets new voltages.

4
00:00:13,154 --> 00:00:17,822
Tuned well, the current settles on a 10 A
step within 0.3 ms.

5
00:00:17,862 --> 00:00:21,077
Push the gains too far, and it rings.

6
00:00:21,117 --> 00:00:25,305
Today: current control in dq, tuned
straight from the motor model.

7
00:00:26,405 --> 00:00:28,601
First, Lesson 18's answers.

8
00:00:28,641 --> 00:00:33,024
One: linear SVPWM reaches the inscribed
circle,

9
00:00:33,064 --> 00:00:37,188
24 V over √3: 13.9 V of phase amplitude.

10
00:00:37,228 --> 00:00:41,373
Two: in the middle of 000, all three low-
side switches conduct,

11
00:00:41,413 --> 00:00:45,227
and the switching edges are as far away as
they get.

12
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:55,957
Three: Kp = ωc L = 2π × 1 kHz × 0.05 mH =
0.31 V/A.

13
00:00:57,057 --> 00:01:01,458
Why control current at all? Because
current is torque:

14
00:01:01,498 --> 00:01:05,066
on our motor, 0.039 N·m per amp of i_q.

15
00:01:05,106 --> 00:01:09,179
Voltage alone doesn't set the current: the
back-EMF takes a share

16
00:01:09,219 --> 00:01:13,376
that grows with speed, and a hot winding's
resistance rises.

17
00:01:13,416 --> 00:01:19,711
At standstill, the full 13.9 V across
40 mΩ would drive 346 A,

18
00:01:19,751 --> 00:01:23,483
17 times the drive's 20 A rating.

19
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:26,624
So we command current instead: an i_q
reference,

20
00:01:26,664 --> 00:01:30,895
clamped at 20 A, that a fast loop makes
the motor follow.

21
00:01:31,995 --> 00:01:39,955
Lesson 14's model is the plant. Each axis
is a resistor and an inductor in series:

22
00:01:39,995 --> 00:01:44,648
1/(R + sL), with one pole at R/L,
800 rad/s.

23
00:01:44,688 --> 00:01:49,350
Rotation adds speed voltages: the back-EMF
ωe λm on q,

24
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:54,800
and a cross-coupling, ωe L times the other
axis's current.

25
00:01:54,840 --> 00:02:01,888
At 3000 rpm, 10 A on q puts 0.63 V on d,
and the back-EMF is 8.17 V.

26
00:02:01,928 --> 00:02:06,702
To each controller, they're disturbances.

27
00:02:07,802 --> 00:02:12,310
The PI controller is a gain plus an
integrator,

28
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:15,904
Kp + Ki/s, with a zero at −Ki/Kp.

29
00:02:15,944 --> 00:02:21,578
Put that zero on the plant's pole, Ki/Kp =
R/L, and the two cancel.

30
00:02:21,618 --> 00:02:26,127
The loop gain becomes Kp/(sL): a pure
integrator.

31
00:02:26,167 --> 00:02:35,525
Choose Kp = ωc L: the loop gain is ωc/s,
and the closed loop is 1/(1 + s/ωc).

32
00:02:35,565 --> 00:02:41,540
First order, with bandwidth ωc. And Ki =
ωc R.

33
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:46,427
For 1 kHz on our motor: Kp is 0.314 V/A,

34
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:53,197
Ki is 251 V/(A·s), and the time constant
is 159 µs.

35
00:02:54,297 --> 00:03:00,328
At speed, the axes talk to each other.
Here's a 10 A step on q at 3000 rpm:

36
00:03:00,368 --> 00:03:06,313
with the two PIs alone, i_d swings to
1.7 A, where we asked for zero.

37
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:11,847
We know those speed voltages, so add them
to the PI outputs:

38
00:03:11,887 --> 00:03:18,980
−ωe L i_q on d, and ωe (L i_d + λm) on q.
That's decoupling feed-forward.

39
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:23,915
Now each axis sees just its R and L again.
The swing drops to 0.6 A;

40
00:03:23,955 --> 00:03:28,156
the rest comes from a delay we haven't
counted yet.

41
00:03:29,256 --> 00:03:34,384
Digital control can't act instantly. The
ADC samples in the middle of 000,

42
00:03:34,424 --> 00:03:39,883
the processor computes, and the new duties
load at the next counter zero.

43
00:03:39,923 --> 00:03:45,271
So the voltage starts one period late, and
it's spread over that period,

44
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:49,412
which adds half a period more: 1.5
samples, 75 µs.

45
00:03:49,452 --> 00:03:53,393
At 1 kHz, 75 µs is 27° of phase lag.

46
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:59,653
The ideal loop had 90° of phase margin;
63° are left.

47
00:03:59,693 --> 00:04:06,266
Simulated sample by sample, the step still
reaches 63 % at 154 µs,

48
00:04:06,306 --> 00:04:09,611
but now overshoots by 1.8 %.

49
00:04:10,711 --> 00:04:14,349
So why not go faster? The delay stays
75 µs,

50
00:04:14,389 --> 00:04:18,009
so a higher crossover loses more phase.

51
00:04:18,049 --> 00:04:22,832
At 1.5 kHz the overshoot is 21 %; at
2 kHz,

52
00:04:22,872 --> 00:04:31,307
a tenth of the PWM frequency, 47 %. Above
3.25 kHz the loop is unstable.

53
00:04:31,347 --> 00:04:36,792
That's why current loops sit at a
twentieth to a tenth of the PWM frequency.

54
00:04:36,832 --> 00:04:40,146
Our 1 kHz at 20 kHz is at the safe end.

55
00:04:41,246 --> 00:04:47,277
One more limit: the voltage. Linear SVPWM
stops at the 13.9 V circle, so the

56
00:04:47,317 --> 00:04:53,987
controller cuts its vector back to it: d
keeps its voltage, q gets what's left.

57
00:04:54,027 --> 00:04:59,345
At 4500 rpm, the back-EMF alone takes
12.3 V. For a 20 A step,

58
00:04:59,385 --> 00:05:04,702
the controller asks for 18.5 V: the vector
sits on the circle,

59
00:05:04,742 --> 00:05:10,460
and the current climbs only as fast as
1.6 V of margin allows.

60
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:15,848
Meanwhile the integrator keeps adding up
an error the voltage can't remove:

61
00:05:15,888 --> 00:05:18,578
2.4 V of it. When the current arrives,

62
00:05:18,618 --> 00:05:23,145
those stored volts push it to 24.2 A, past
the 20 A limit.

63
00:05:23,185 --> 00:05:28,343
Anti-windup fixes it: feed the cut-off
part back, so the integrator only

64
00:05:28,383 --> 00:05:33,937
stores what the inverter really applies.
The overshoot drops to 0.5 %.

65
00:05:35,037 --> 00:05:40,857
Here's the whole loop, every 50 µs: two
shunts sampled, Clarke and Park with the

66
00:05:40,897 --> 00:05:46,751
rotor angle, the PIs with feed-forward,
the limit, inverse Park, and SVPWM.

67
00:05:46,791 --> 00:05:53,940
Simulated edge by edge at 3000 rpm, the
phase currents carry 2.8 A of PWM ripple,

68
00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:59,399
yet the samples land within 0.5 mA of the
averaged model.

69
00:05:59,439 --> 00:06:01,978
So the averaged model is the one to tune
with;

70
00:06:02,018 --> 00:06:04,872
the switched one shows ripple and sensing.

71
00:06:05,972 --> 00:06:10,688
Your turn. Compute Kp and Ki from your own
R and L,

72
00:06:10,728 --> 00:06:18,082
measured as in Lesson 15; in code, store
Ki × Ts, 0.0126 V/A for our motor.

73
00:06:18,122 --> 00:06:23,260
Then step i_d from 0 to 5 A at standstill.
On a surface-magnet rotor,

74
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:27,787
i_d makes no torque, so it stays put. Log
every sample.

75
00:06:27,827 --> 00:06:34,878
Expect two samples of nothing, the delay,
then 1.5, 3.1, 4.1 and 4.7 A,

76
00:06:34,918 --> 00:06:43,209
peaking at 5.09. More overshoot means a
high estimate: L 30 % high gives 10 %.

77
00:06:44,309 --> 00:06:50,524
Finally, one iteration in C, run by the
ADC interrupt in the middle of 000.

78
00:06:50,564 --> 00:06:54,563
Scale the shunt readings, then Clarke, and
Park with the rotor angle.

79
00:06:54,603 --> 00:06:57,224
Then the PIs plus feed-forward, the limit,

80
00:06:57,264 --> 00:07:00,855
d first, and the integrators with back-
calculation.

81
00:07:00,895 --> 00:07:07,334
Last, inverse Park, at the angle the rotor
will have when the voltage lands,

82
00:07:07,374 --> 00:07:11,341
1.5 samples later: 5.4° at 3000 rpm. Skip
that,

83
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:15,236
and our step's d swing doubles, to 1.3 A.

84
00:07:15,276 --> 00:07:20,693
Space-vector PWM writes the compare
registers, and they load at the next zero.

85
00:07:21,793 --> 00:07:24,008
Three questions before next time.

86
00:07:24,048 --> 00:07:25,799
One: with Kp = ωc L and Ki = ωc R,

87
00:07:25,839 --> 00:07:29,026
what does the ideal closed-loop response
look like?

88
00:07:30,026 --> 00:07:37,214
Two: sampling at 20 kHz with a 1.5-sample
delay, how much phase lag at 1 kHz?

89
00:07:38,214 --> 00:07:41,188
Three: a speed loop sits on top of this
current loop.

90
00:07:41,228 --> 00:07:44,173
How much lower is its bandwidth,
typically?

91
00:07:45,173 --> 00:07:47,171
Next time: field-oriented control,

92
00:07:47,211 --> 00:07:50,827
the whole cascade from speed down to these
currents.
